<%# locals: (no_questions: false) %>

<%#
  Questions about the Fuime agreement.

  This replaced HCB's fiscal-sponsorship FAQ, which described a 501(c)(3)
  relationship Fuime does not have: IP assigned to Hack Club's EIN, IRS
  Determination Letters, nonprofit asset transfer on termination. None of it
  applied to a teen-business platform, and leaving it beside a signature field
  would have explained the wrong document.

  Keep this consistent with the agreement users actually sign. If the DocuSeal
  template changes, re-read this file — an FAQ that contradicts the contract
  next to it is worse than no FAQ.
%>

<%= render "application/callout", title: "Questions about the Fuime agreement?", type: "info", class: "my-0", footer: no_questions ? nil : :questions do %>
  <p>
    <span class="font-bold">Why do I need to sign this agreement?</span><br>
    It sets the terms for using Fuime to run your business — what you can expect
    from us, and what we expect from you. You need to accept it before your
    business can move money on the platform.
  </p>

  <p>
    <span class="font-bold">Does Fuime own my business?</span><br>
    No. Your business is yours. Fuime is the software you run it on, not a
    parent company, an owner, or a shareholder. Nothing you sign here transfers
    ownership of your business, your work, or your ideas.
  </p>

  <p>
    <span class="font-bold">Who owns what I make?</span><br>
    You do. Anything you create — code, designs, products, branding — stays
    yours, both while you use Fuime and after you leave.
  </p>

  <p>
    <span class="font-bold">Why does my parent or guardian have to sign?</span><br>
    Everyone running a business on Fuime is under 18, so a parent or legal
    guardian has to agree too. They are the legal signer, they can see your
    business activity, and they can withdraw consent at any time. This is a
    requirement of the platform, not a setting you or they can turn off.
  </p>

  <p>
    <span class="font-bold">What about taxes?</span><br>
    Money your business earns may create tax obligations. Fuime keeps your
    records and gives you summaries to work from, but Fuime is a software
    platform and does not file your taxes or give tax or legal advice. For
    anything specific to your situation, talk to a professional.
  </p>

  <p>
    <span class="font-bold">Can I leave?</span><br>
    Yes, at any time. Your business, your money, and everything you have made
    are yours to take with you.
  </p>
<% end %>
